  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:769-834  sbi_hart_switch_mode
      This is the most direct YMODEW analog in the codebase. The function modifies architectural processor state — MSTATUS.MPP (privilege mode), MEPC (execution target), and virtualization mode (MPV) — then executes mret to commit the transition. The only validation on next_mode is an ISA extension presence check (misa_extension('S') / misa_extension('U')) — a single-bit condition, analogous to checking only the 'sealed' bit on a CHERI capability. There is no capability-based authorization: no tag validity check, no bounds verification on next_addr, no permission validation, no sealing chain. The next_addr is written directly to MEPC without any integrity verification. Currently safe because all callers source next_mode/next_addr from domain metadata that was sanitized at registration time, but there is no runtime re-validation. The implicit invariant — that sanitized domain metadata remains unmodified between registration and mret — is not enforced in code.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:102-169  switch_to_next_domain_context
      The SATP csr_swap at line 139 is the critical YMODEW-analogous write — it atomically swaps the page table root, ASID, and address translation mode, which architecturally governs all memory access for the hart. The only validation before this write is a non-NULL + distinctness check (line 110). There is no integrity verification on the SATP value: no check that the mode field encodes a valid translation scheme, no ASID bounds check, no PPN bounds verification against physical memory. PMP configuration is blindly reconfigured (lines 127-128) and the full S-mode trap context (sstatus, sie, stvec, sscratch, sepc, scause, stval, sip) is blindly swapped (lines 131-139). No authorization verifies that the current domain is permitted to transition to the target domain. In CHERI terms: the non-NULL check is the 'sealed bit' gate, and the missing full integrity validation encompasses SATP field validation, domain transition authorization, and trap-context consistency.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hsm.c:139-158  sbi_hsm_hart_start_finish
      The state transition gate at lines 148-149 (START_PENDING → STARTED) is the sole integrity barrier before consuming architectural state values (next_addr, next_mode, next_arg1) from scratch and passing them to sbi_hart_switch_mode. This is the 'sealed bit only' pattern exactly: the state (seal analog) is verified but the payload — the values that will become architectural processor state — is not integrity-checked. The scratch values were written earlier (in sbi_hsm_hart_start or sbi_domain_startup) and could theoretically have been corrupted between write and consume within M-mode. In practice, both producer and consumer run in M-mode within the same trusted firmware, but the code pattern matches YMODEW vulnerability: a state-machine flag is the only gate before a privilege-mode-defining operation.
